steps.body:
A feed handler needs two abilities that other code already has: a name for logs and dashboards, from a small `Named` class with `name()`, and a count of the messages it has handled, from a small `Counted` class with `bump()` and `count()`. Rather than write both again, it inherits both. List the bases after the colon, separated by commas:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class Named {
public:
    explicit Named(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {}
    const std::string& name() const { return name_; }

private:
    std::string name_;
};

class Counted {
public:
    void bump() { ++count_; }
    long long count() const { return count_; }

private:
    long long count_ = 0;
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    Feed feed("AAPL");
    feed.bump();
    feed.bump();
    std::cout << feed.name() << ' ' << feed.count() << ' ' << sizeof(Feed) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
class Feed : public Named, public Counted {
public:
    explicit Feed(const std::string& name) : Named(name) {}
};
```

- Each base takes its own access word. In `class Feed : public Named, Counted`, the second base gets the default, which is private for a `class`, so outside code can't call `feed.bump()`.
- The bases are built left to right in the order of the base list, then the members, then the constructor body. The initializer list's order doesn't change that, and clang's `-Wall` on a Mac warns when the two disagree: `base class 'Counted' will be initialized after base 'Named'`. Destruction runs in reverse.
- A Feed holds a whole Named followed by a whole Counted, so `sizeof(Feed)` is 32 + 8 = 40 here. On a Mac it's 24 + 8, since libc++'s `std::string` is 24 bytes.

A Feed converts to a `Named&` and to a `Counted&`, and each must refer to its own part. The Named part starts where the Feed does, but the Counted part starts 32 bytes in, so converting a Feed's address to a `Counted*` changes it, and the compiler does that for you. Here are three functions whose body is `return f;`, from the sandbox's g++ 10.2 at `-O2`, where `rdi` holds the argument and `rax` the result:

```text
_Z8as_namedPK4Feed:            # const Feed* to const Named*
        mov     rax, rdi       # the same address
        ret
_Z10as_countedPK4Feed:         # const Feed* to const Counted*
        lea     rax, [rdi+32]  # 32 bytes in
        test    rdi, rdi
        mov     edx, 0
        cmove   rax, rdx       # but a null pointer stays null
        ret
_Z14as_counted_refRK4Feed:     # const Feed& to const Counted&
        lea     rax, [rdi+32]  # a reference is never null
        ret
```

A Mac's clang does the same with `add x8, x0, #24`. That hidden adjustment is why you convert between related classes through their types and never by reinterpreting an address, which the next lesson, *Conversions and Casts*, comes back to.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Named {
public:
    explicit Named(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {
        std::cout << "Named " << name_ << '\n';
    }
    ~Named() { std::cout << "~Named\n"; }
    const std::string& name() const { return name_; }

private:
    std::string name_;
};

class Counted {
public:
    Counted() { std::cout << "Counted\n"; }
    ~Counted() { std::cout << "~Counted\n"; }
    void bump() { ++count_; }
    long long count() const { return count_; }

private:
    long long count_ = 0;
};

class Feed : public Named, public Counted {
public:
    explicit Feed(const std::string& name) : Named(name) {
        std::cout << "Feed " << name << '\n';
    }
    ~Feed() { std::cout << "~Feed\n"; }
};

void show_name(const Named& n) { std::cout << "name: " << n.name() << '\n'; }
void show_count(const Counted& c) { std::cout << "count: " << c.count() << '\n'; }

int main() {
    Feed feed("AAPL");
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        feed.bump();
    }
    show_name(feed);
    show_count(feed);
    std::cout << "sizeof " << sizeof(Named) << " + " << sizeof(Counted) << " = " << sizeof(Feed) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

