steps.body:
Two bases written by different teams often pick the same names. A `Trader` logs in through a `Session`, which has an `id()`, and trades for an `Account`, which has an `id()` too. The class compiles, and the first call to `id()` doesn't:

```cpp error
class Session {
public:
    int id() const { return 7; }
};

class Account {
public:
    int id() const { return 42; }
};

class Trader : public Session, public Account {};

Trader t;
std::cout << t.id() << '\n';  // error: request for member 'id' is ambiguous
```

g++ lists both candidates, `int Account::id() const` and `int Session::id() const`. The compiler finds a name in every base before it looks at the arguments, so the clash happens even when the signatures differ: an `id()` in one base and an `id(int)` in the other are just as ambiguous. There are three ways out:

- **Qualify the call.** `t.Session::id()` and `t.Account::id()` name the base, and inside Trader's own functions, `Session::id()` does the same.
- **Pick one with a using declaration.** `using Account::id;` inside Trader's body brings Account's `id` into Trader's scope, so a plain `t.id()` means the account's, while `t.Session::id()` still reaches the other.
- **Write the name in Trader.** A member of the derived class hides that name in every base, and its body can call each base's version by its qualified name.

A reference to one base sees only that base. A `const Session&` bound to a Trader calls Session's `id()`, with nothing to resolve.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

class Session {
public:
    explicit Session(int id) : id_(id) {}
    int id() const { return id_; }
    void reset() { std::cout << "session " << id_ << " logged out\n"; }

private:
    int id_;
};

class Account {
public:
    explicit Account(int id) : id_(id) {}
    int id() const { return id_; }
    void deposit(long long cents) { cash_ += cents; }
    void reset() {
        std::cout << "account " << id_ << " cleared " << cash_ << " cents\n";
        cash_ = 0;
    }

private:
    int id_;
    long long cash_ = 0;
};

class Trader : public Session, public Account {
public:
    Trader(int session, int account) : Session(session), Account(account) {}
    using Account::id;  // a plain id() means the account's
    void reset() {      // hides both bases' reset(), then runs each one
        Session::reset();
        Account::reset();
    }
};

void show_session(const Session& s) { std::cout << "session id " << s.id() << '\n'; }

int main() {
    Trader t(7, 42);
    t.deposit(150000);
    std::cout << "id " << t.id() << '\n';
    std::cout << "Session::id " << t.Session::id() << '\n';
    show_session(t);
    t.reset();
    return 0;
}

