#include <iostream>

class Primary {
public:
    void send(int qty) {
        std::cout << "primary " << qty << '\n';
        sent_ += qty;
    }
    long long sent() const { return sent_; }

private:
    long long sent_ = 0;
};

class Backup {
public:
    void send(int qty) {
        std::cout << "backup " << qty << '\n';
        sent_ += qty;
    }
    long long sent() const { return sent_; }

private:
    long long sent_ = 0;
};

class Router : public Primary, public Backup {
public:
    using Primary::send;
    long long sent() const { return Primary::sent() + Backup::sent(); }
};

int main() {
    int limit = 0;
    std::cin >> limit;
    Router router;
    int qty = 0;
    while (std::cin >> qty) {
        if (qty <= limit) {
            router.send(qty);
        } else {
            router.Backup::send(qty);
        }
    }
    std::cout << "primary sent " << router.Primary::sent() << '\n';
    std::cout << "backup sent " << router.Backup::sent() << '\n';
    std::cout << "total sent " << router.sent() << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
An instrument library has an `Asset` base that holds a symbol. `Listed` adds a lot size and `Priced` a last price, each deriving from Asset. A `Stock` is both, so it derives from both, and now it holds two Asset parts, each with its own copy of the symbol. That shape is the **diamond**:

```text
       Asset
      /     \
  Listed   Priced
      \     /
       Stock
```

With plain bases, building a Stock runs Asset's constructor twice, and anything that needs *the* Asset is ambiguous: `stock.symbol()`, or passing a Stock as a `const Asset&`, which gives `error: 'Asset' is an ambiguous base of 'Stock'`.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class Asset {
public:
    explicit Asset(const std::string& symbol) : symbol_(symbol) {}
    const std::string& symbol() const { return symbol_; }

private:
    std::string symbol_;
};

class Listed : public Asset {
public:
    explicit Listed(const std::string& symbol) : Asset(symbol) {}
};

class Priced : public Asset {
public:
    explicit Priced(const std::string& symbol) : Asset(symbol) {}
};

class Stock : public Listed, public Priced {
public:
    explicit Stock(const std::string& symbol) : Listed(symbol), Priced(symbol) {}
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp error
const Stock stock("AAPL");
std::cout << stock.symbol() << '\n';  // error: request for member 'symbol' is ambiguous
```

**Virtual inheritance** shares the base. With `class Listed : public virtual Asset`, and the same for Priced, a Stock has exactly one Asset part. Two rules come with it:

- The class you create, the *most derived* one, builds the virtual base itself, before any other base. Stock's initializer list calls `Asset(symbol)`, and the `Asset(...)` calls in Listed's and Priced's lists are skipped. Leave that call out of Stock's list, and g++ looks for a default constructor: `no matching function for call to 'Asset::Asset()'`.
- A Listed made on its own is the most derived class there, so its own `Asset(symbol)` runs.

The price is size and speed. Listed can't know where the shared Asset will sit, since that depends on the final class, so each class that inherits virtually carries a hidden pointer to a table that holds the offset. For an Asset that holds only a `long long id_`, these sizes are the same with g++ here and clang on a Mac:

| Class | Plain bases | Virtual bases |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Asset | 8 | 8 |
| Listed, adding an `int` | 16 | 24 |
| Stock | 32, two Assets | 40, one Asset |

Reading `id_` through a `const Listed&` takes one load with a plain base and three with a virtual one, since the offset comes from the table first. `VListed` is the virtual version, from g++ 10.2 at `-O2`:

```text
_Z8id_plainRK6Listed:
        mov     rax, QWORD PTR [rdi]       # id_, at offset 0
        ret
_Z10id_virtualRK7VListed:
        mov     rax, QWORD PTR [rdi]       # the hidden table pointer
        mov     rax, QWORD PTR [rax-24]    # where Asset is, from the table
        mov     rax, QWORD PTR [rdi+rax]   # then id_
        ret
```

The standard library's `std::iostream`, a stream you both read and write, is a diamond: it derives from `std::istream` and `std::ostream`, which share one `std::ios` virtually, so the stream has one state and one buffer.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Asset {
public:
    explicit Asset(const std::string& symbol) : symbol_(symbol) {
        std::cout << "Asset " << symbol_ << '\n';
    }
    const std::string& symbol() const { return symbol_; }

private:
    std::string symbol_;
};

class Listed : public virtual Asset {
public:
    Listed(const std::string& symbol, int lot) : Asset(symbol), lot_(lot) {
        std::cout << "Listed lot " << lot_ << '\n';
    }
    int lot() const { return lot_; }

private:
    int lot_;
};

class Priced : public virtual Asset {
public:
    Priced(const std::string& symbol, double px) : Asset(symbol), px_(px) {
        std::cout << "Priced " << px_ << '\n';
    }
    double px() const { return px_; }

private:
    double px_;
};

class Stock : public Listed, public Priced {
public:
    Stock(const std::string& symbol, int lot, double px)
        : Asset(symbol), Listed(symbol, lot), Priced(symbol, px) {
        std::cout << "Stock " << symbol << '\n';
    }
    double lot_value() const { return lot() * px(); }
};

void show(const Asset& a) { std::cout << "asset " << a.symbol() << '\n'; }

int main() {
    const Stock stock("AAPL", 100, 192.5);
    show(stock);
    std::cout << stock.symbol() << " lot value " << stock.lot_value() << '\n';
    std::cout << "--\n";
    const Listed alone("MSFT", 10);
    show(alone);
    return 0;
}

